els":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"## What went wrong\n\nOnly pair 1 failed, and the error was small: 3 cells where the program drew a 1 but the output has background 8. All three sit on the right edge of the canvas in its bottom rows.\n\nThe cause is that the two shapes' bounding boxes overlap. In pair 1 the empty outline's box spans columns 1 to 11 and rows 2 to 12. The coloured template begins at column 10, and some of its line cells (input row 10, column 11, for example) fall inside that box. My crop kept every cell of the line colour inside the box. So a few line cells from the template leaked into the canvas.\n\nThe fix is to rebuild each crop from its own object's cells only. Everything else in the box becomes background. I apply this to the template crop as well, so its pockets cannot pick up cells from the outline either. Pair 2 was already exact, and nothing about the matching rule changes.\n\n## The rule, restated\n\n1. **Two shapes, one line colour.** Each input holds a *template*, whose enclosed pockets are coloured, and an *empty outline* of the same design with different proportions. They are separated as diagonal-connected multicolour objects. The template is the one with the most colours. The outline uses a single colour.\n2. **Canvas.** The output is the outline's bounding box. It holds only that object's own line cells, with background everywhere else. This removes the overlap problem above.\n3. **Pockets.** In each cleaned crop, the components of non-line cells are the pockets. A pure-background component that touches the crop edge is the exterior and is ignored.\n4. **Matching.** Each pocket gets a centroid, normalised by its shape's height and width. Each outline pocket is flooded with the majority colour of the template pocket whose normalised centroid is nearest. Enclosed background in the template stays background. This is region filling, not rescaling: in pair 1 the template's T-shaped 7 pocket becomes a solid 2×3 block.\n\n**Test inputs.**\n- Test 1: the colour-4 drawing at the bottom right is filled from the multicoloured template at the top left.\n- Test 2: the colour-2 drawing on the left is filled from the template on the right.\n\n**Emitted stages.** First the clean outline canvas, then one stage per fill colour, then the final grid.","code":"import collections\n\ndef transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    obs = [o for o in objects(g, bg=bg, diagonal=True, multicolour=True) if o['size'] > 8]\n\n    def ncols(o):\n        return len(set(int(g[y, x]) for y, x in o['cells']))\n\n    obs.sort(key=lambda o: (ncols(o), -o['size']))\n    outline = obs[0]\n    template = max(obs[1:], key=ncols)\n    line = int(g[outline['cells'][0]])\n\n    def own_crop(o):\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = o['box']\n        c = np.full((y1 - y0 + 1, x1 - x0 + 1), bg, dtype=g.dtype)\n        for y, x in o['cells']:\n            c[y - y0, x - x0] = g[y, x]\n        return c\n\n    def pockets(c):\n        H, W = c.shape\n        res = []\n        for comp in components(c != line):\n            vals = [int(c[y, x]) for y, x in comp]\n            border = any(y in (0, H - 1) or x in (0, W - 1) for y, x in comp)\n            if border and all(v == bg for v in vals):\n                continue\n            col = collections.Counter(vals).most_common(1)[0][0]\n            cy = np.mean([y for y, x in comp]) / max(H - 1, 1)\n            cx = np.mean([x for y, x in comp]) / max(W - 1, 1)\n            res.append((comp, col, cy, cx))\n        return res\n\n    oc = own_crop(outline)\n    tc = own_crop(template)\n    out = np.where(oc == line, line, bg)\n    emit(out, \"Crop the empty outline (only its own line cells) as the canvas.\")\n\n    tp = pockets(tc)\n    op = pockets(out)\n    fills = collections.defaultdict(list)\n    for comp, _, cy, cx in op:\n        best = min(tp, key=lambda t: (t[2] - cy) ** 2 + (t[3] - cx) ** 2)\n        fills[best[1]].append(comp)\n    for col in sorted(fills):\n        if col == bg:\n            continue\n        for comp in fills[col]:\n            for y, x in comp:\n                out[y, x] = col\n        emit(out, f\"Fill outline pockets matching template pockets of colour {col}.\")\n    emit(out, \"Final: outline with every pocket coloured like its template counterpart.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"template":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The coloured template begins at column 10, and some of its line cells (input row 10, column 11, for example) fall inside that box."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"So a few line cells from the template leaked into the canvas."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"I apply this to the template crop as well, so its pockets cannot pick up cells from the outline either."}],"topology":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Two shapes, one line colour.** Each input holds a *template*, whose enclosed pockets are coloured, and an *empty outline* of the same design with different proportions."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Each outline pocket is flooded with the majority colour of the template pocket whose normalised centroid is nearest."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Enclosed background in the template stays background."}],"dynamics":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"red template begins at column 10, and some of its line cells (input row 10, column 11, for example) fall inside that box."}],"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The cause is that the two shapes' bounding boxes overlap."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"My crop kept every cell of the line colour inside the box."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The fix is to rebuild each crop from its own object's cells only."}],"layers":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The cause is that the two shapes' bounding boxes overlap."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"This removes the overlap problem above."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for